From d05c24ff9fa7e903f9d09ceda155a782c83bdd1d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 04:54:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(rlsm): stamp preprocess mode and scale on every OCR
 observation
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

ocr_observations recorded psm and engine_version but not which preprocessing
produced the text, so nothing in the database distinguished a row read from a
raw crop from one read after binarizing at 2x.

That matters because resume keys on screenshots.ocr_status: a screenshot marked
'ok' is never re-read. Change a zone's mode or scale and every older row stays
in place — worse than the new ones, indistinguishable from them, and invisible
in the coverage report. The only way to answer "is this row current?" was to
correlate observed_at against what the code looked like that week.

  - data/rlsm/schema.sql gains nullable `preprocess` and `preprocess_scale`.
  - ensure_observation_columns() ALTERs them onto existing databases; nullable
    keeps every existing row valid, reading as "unknown preprocessing", which
    is exactly what it is.
  - Both runners stamp the resolved (mode, scale) beside psm at write time.
  - Preflight counts observations_needing_preprocess — the newest label_layer
    row per screenshot whose stamp differs from ZONE_OCR_CONFIG — and warns.
  - rlsm_ocr_parallel --reocr-stale-preprocess re-reads exactly those, and the
    pipeline runs it as a third pass when preflight reports a nonzero count.
    Same shape as the existing --reocr-boxes backfill: raw OCR is never
    overwritten, new rows append under a fresh run_id.

Judging staleness on the newest row per screenshot is what keeps a re-read from
looping: the superseded row stays in the table but no longer selects.

13 new tests cover the migration (including idempotency), the staleness query
across mode, scale, unstamped rows, newest-row-wins and zone isolation, and
config_stamp against the live zone config. 72 pass overall.

